# A timer was created on hardware, 2026-07-27:
#   16:20, action = turn the lamp blue, repeat = once.
#
# Changed keys:
#   key 3:  False -> True          (timer enable, most likely)
#   key 41/42/43: headers changed IDENTICALLY, segments -> all zero
#   key 80: [1,10] -> [0,10]       (gradual manually reset to baseline)
#
# NOT FOUND anywhere in the 22 readable properties:
#   58800 (16:20 as seconds)   980 (as minutes)   16,20 (as bytes)
#   any blue value             any repeat/once flag
#
# So a timer's content joins mode control and sunrise/sunset behind the
# truncated read. Only its enable flag is visible.
#
# CAUTION, a coincidence that looks like a finding: key 24's header reads
# "10 14 ..." = 16, 20, which resembles the timer time exactly. It is not.
# Byte 1 of key 24 has been 20 in every capture ever taken because it is the
# segment count, and byte 0 varies with mode. Verified against the session-1
# and music-mode captures before reporting.
#
# DECISIVE FOLLOW-UP: the timer was then edited to a clean single-variable
# change, 16:20 -> 12:34, action on, mood light all blue 100%, repeat once.
#
# ZERO keys changed. Not the time, not a checksum, not a revision counter.
# Searched 45240 (12:34 as seconds), 754 (as minutes), the byte pairs 12,34 and
# 34,12, and every 16- and 32-bit window LE and BE across all 22 properties.
#
# Two conclusions:
#  1. Timer content is entirely invisible to readable state. Only key 3, the
#     enable flag, ever moved.
#  2. The key 41/42/43 changes in the previous round were therefore NOT caused
#     by the timer. They coincided with it and were wrongly attributed. Their
#     real trigger is unidentified.
